    meditation: `There is a moment in nearly every practice when the statute, read straight through, will produce a result that no honest person would call just. The text is plain. The facts fit it as a key fits a lock. And still, applying the rule will work an injury the legislature plainly never imagined. This is the moment the Christian advocate inherits a problem older than common law and older than the Code: the problem of the general rule confronted by the particular case.

Aquinas is not afraid of it. "The lawgiver cannot have in view every single case," he writes, and so he "shapes the law according to what happens most frequently." That is what a statute is: a portrait of the ordinary. It is drafted with the typical defendant in mind, the typical contract, the typical accident, the typical family. But there are no typical defendants in your office today. There is only the one in front of you, with her particular history and her particular fear, and the question she has come to ask is whether the law has anything to say to her case that is not cruel.

The Lord answers the Pharisees in the same temper. They have caught the disciples plucking grain on the Sabbath and they have a statute. The statute is real; it is not a fiction; it is Sinai itself. And Jesus does not deny the statute. He quotes the prophet against the misuse of it. "If you knew what this meaneth: I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: you would never have condemned the innocent." The condemnation comes not from the law but from a way of reading the law that has forgotten what the law was for. The Sabbath was given for man, and the man before them is hungry. The rule, applied without remembering its purpose, has become an instrument against the very life it was given to protect.

You will be tempted, as you read this, to think that equity is a soft virtue, a kind of moral leniency that lets the well-meaning lawyer fudge the edges of the rule. It is not. Equity is harder than the rule. The rule asks only that you read; equity asks that you understand. The rule can be applied at scale; equity must be applied to a person. The rule fits in a checklist; equity will not fit anywhere except inside a conscience that has been formed by long attention. To stand in the gap between the statute and the case is to do a more difficult thing than to apply the statute, because you must know the statute well enough to see what it was reaching for, and you must know the case well enough to see where its reaching falls short.

Consider the boilerplate motion you will file this week. It is a form. Someone you never met drafted the original, and you have changed three names and a docket number and the citation to a case. The form is, in itself, a small monument to legal economy: it is what allows one lawyer to serve many clients without inventing the wheel for each of them. But it is also a danger, because the form does not know your client. The form has a typical client in mind, and your client is not typical. Read the form again before you sign it and ask whether anything in this particular case requires a sentence the form does not contain. Most days the answer will be no. The day the answer is yes, you have met equity, and you owe it the extra hour.

Consider the misdemeanor docket call. Forty cases before lunch. The judge has a script and the prosecutor has a script and the public defender, if your client is fortunate enough to have one, has been handed a file ten minutes ago. The system is built for volume. It is, again, not a wicked thing in itself: the alternative — that every shoplifting case receive the attention of a homicide — would collapse the courts within a week. But the volume creates a temptation to forget that each case is a case. The defendant who is third on the list is not a unit; she is a woman whose mother is in the hospital and whose children are in school and whose job will not survive a conviction. The statute does not know any of this. You may be the only person in the room whose office it is to tell the court that the statute does not know.

Aquinas calls the virtue by its Greek name, epieikeia — the right reading of the rule. It is not the suspension of the rule; it is the rule's own honesty about what it is. A statute that pretended to anticipate every case would be either tyrannical or absurd. The statute is humble; it knows it is general. Equity is the courtesy the law shows itself by remembering that humility in the courtroom. The advocate who pleads equity is not pleading against the legislature; he is pleading on behalf of the legislature, defending what the lawmakers would have said had they been told this particular story.

Take this, then, into the day. The forms will pile up. The dockets will move. The pressure to bill the hour and close the file is real, and you cannot make it vanish by being holy about it. But somewhere in the stack will be one case where the typical answer is not the true answer, and the question for you is whether you will see it. The whole vocation, in a sense, is the practice of seeing it. The law schools do not teach you to see it; the bar exam does not test for it; the partners' compensation formula does not reward it. You will learn it, if you learn it at all, from cases. And from prayer, which is the slow training of attention.

"I will have mercy, and not sacrifice." It is not a license to break the rule. It is a warning against the kind of reading that makes the rule break people.`,
