    meditation: `St. German was writing in 1530, in the small hinge of a dialogue between a Doctor of Divinity and a Student of the Laws of England. He was trying to teach the student something that the bar exam still does not teach: that equity is not a separate court invented by chancellors but a faculty of the law itself. "Equity," he says, "is a righteousness that considereth all the particular circumstances of the deed, the which also is tempered with the sweetness of mercy." The whole sentence is worth slow reading. Equity is a righteousness. It is not a softening of righteousness, not its opposite. It is righteousness done in the only way that fully serves its name: with the particular case in view.

The prophet Micheas says the same thing from a different angle. "What doth the Lord require of thee?" — and then three things in apposition, not three separate duties but one duty refracted into three lights. To do judgment. To love mercy. To walk solicitous with thy God. Judgment without mercy is what Pharaoh did in Egypt; mercy without judgment is what Eli did with his sons. The third clause, walking solicitous with God, is the discipline by which the first two stay married. To do judgment and to love mercy is the work of one undivided heart, and equity, in our trade, is the legal name for that undividedness.

Notice the structure of St. German's argument. He does not say that equity steps in when the law has run out. He says that equity must be observed in every law and in every general rule of it. Equity is not a backup system. It is the way every general rule is meant to be read. The rule and its equity are like the body and the breath: separate them and you no longer have a living creature. The lawyer who thinks of equity as a special remedy for unusual cases has misunderstood what equity is. Equity is the ordinary mode of the lawful mind.

He quotes, beautifully, the wise man: "Be not over much righteous; for the extreme righteousness is extreme wrong." It is the same warning Cicero put as summum ius summa iniuria — the highest law, the highest injury. The lawyer who is most certain that he is right is the lawyer most in danger of doing the most serious wrong. The certainty is a sign that the case has been compressed into the rule before it was understood. The compression is what does the harm. The form contract that solves ninety-nine cases and ruins one is, for the one, an instrument of the highest injury, precisely because it carried the highest law into a place the law was not made for.

Consider what it means for a statute to remember its own purpose. A statute is a sentence written by people for people. It came into being because of a problem. Someone, at some point, saw an injustice and tried to prevent its recurrence by laying down a general rule. The rule was a means. The purpose was the prevention of the injustice. Over time, the rule begins to lead a life of its own; it is cited and re-cited; it accumulates a commentary; it becomes, in the legal imagination, a thing whose authority no longer requires reference to the injustice that prompted it. This is when statutes go bad. They go bad not by being violated but by being applied without memory.

You have seen this. The fee schedule that was promulgated to ensure widows got their probate inheritance promptly, now used to extract twice the fee from a widow whose case is simple. The expedited eviction process that was designed for genuinely abandoned units, now used against tenants who answered the door. The mandatory minimum that was passed in response to a particular crime wave, now applied to the seventy-year-old grandmother whose case bears no resemblance to anything the legislature pictured. In each case the rule is intact. In each case the purpose has been forgotten. The injustice the rule was meant to prevent is now being committed by the rule.

The Christian advocate has a particular calling here, and it is not a sentimental one. It is the calling to be the memory of the law. The judge cannot hold every legislative history in mind. The prosecutor will not, in the heat of the docket, recall the floor debate. The client does not know any of it. You are the one who has been trained, sometimes at appalling expense, to read the statute against its purpose. You are the one whose name on the brief obliges you to ask, in every case, not only what the rule says but what the rule was for. When you do this, you are not opposing the law. You are doing for the law what no one else in the courtroom has the standing to do. You are loving it back to itself.

This is harder than it sounds, because the legal culture rewards the opposite. It rewards the citation, the brief, the brightness of the line. To plead purpose feels, often, like pleading at a different register, as if you had switched from prose to poetry mid-argument. The judge will sometimes resent it. Opposing counsel will treat it as weakness, the lawyer's confession that he has no case on the text. You will need a particular kind of nerve. You will need to remember, on those mornings, that the law was made for man and not man for the law, and that the courtroom that has forgotten this has not become more lawful but less.

Walk solicitous with God today. The word in Hebrew suggests careful attention, the kind of walk that watches its steps. Equity is the lawyer's version of that walk. Watch your steps in the cases. Read the rule, and then read again for its purpose. The judgment and the mercy were never two things; they were one thing, and your office is to keep them so.`,
