    meditation: `Selden was no enemy of equity; he was a friend of measure. His jibe about the chancellor's foot is the most quoted line in the literature on equity, and it deserves its fame, because it names the discipline equity owes the law. If equity is only the conscience of the particular judge — if it is whatever this chancellor, on this morning, with this digestion, happens to think fair — then it is not a virtue but a mood. A mood with the force of a decree behind it is a frightening thing. Selden's joke is a warning that has lost none of its bite in three hundred and fifty years.

The warning matters because the temptation it names is real. You will encounter, in your career, judges whose sense of fairness is essentially their sense of themselves. You will encounter advocates who plead equity whenever the text is against them, as if the word were a wild card to be played from the back of the hand. You will, in your own work, feel the pull. The text is hard; the case is sympathetic; you would like to argue that the rule should bend. Selden is asking you, before you draft the brief, what discipline your argument is under. Whose foot will set the standard? Yours? The judge's? The client's tears? Or something more durable than any of these?

The book of Proverbs gives the answer in a single line. "To do mercy and judgment, pleaseth the Lord more than victims." Mercy and judgment, again together, again inseparable. The verse does not exalt mercy over judgment; it does not exalt judgment over mercy; it exalts the doing of them as a single pleasing thing, more pleasing than the elaborate ritual that pretends to substitute for either. The "victims" in the Hebrew are the sacrifices of the temple, the costly performance of religion. The proverb says, in effect: do not bring me the brief that performs righteousness without doing it. The chancellor whose foot is his own appetite is performing equity. He is not doing it. The doing has a standard, and the standard is older than him.

The medieval chancellors knew this, although Selden's joke obscures it. The Chancery in its great period was an ecclesiastical court before it was a royal one. The Lord Chancellor was usually a bishop, trained in canon law, accountable not only to the king but to a confessor and, behind the confessor, to a tradition that took conscience as a matter for examination, not improvisation. Conscience in that tradition is not what you feel; it is the application of a known good to the case before you. It can be ill-formed; it can be malformed; it requires training. The chancellor's foot was supposed to be a foot disciplined by Scripture, by the Fathers, by the canons, by the moral theology in which he had been catechized for thirty years before he ever set foot in Westminster. That is a foot of a particular size. It is not the foot of the chancellor's mood.

The Christian advocate is the heir of that discipline, and the heir is supposed to know what was bequeathed. When you plead equity in a brief, you are not pleading your sentiment. You are pleading a tradition. You are pleading Aquinas and Aristotle and Micheas and James. You are pleading the long argument of a civilization that has thought carefully about when the rule should bend and why, and you owe it to the court to do that argument in public, with citations and reasoning and a clear account of the limits of what you are asking. You owe the court an equity that is not a roguish thing. The chancellor's foot is not a problem if the foot has been measured.

There is a parallel discipline you owe in your own life. The lawyer who is honest with himself knows that he has favored clients. There are people he wants to help; there are people he does not. There are cases that move him; there are cases that bore him. Equity becomes roguish when these inclinations begin to determine which cases get the careful work and which get the boilerplate. You may not realize you are doing it. The sympathetic case gets the late hours; the unsympathetic case gets the form motion; the small case for the difficult client gets the intern. This is the chancellor's foot in your own office, and you are the chancellor. The discipline of equity begins at home, with an honest account of where your foot is unequal.

Selden's quip is also a warning against a certain rhetoric that has become common in our time, the rhetoric that treats every appeal to rules as a failure of compassion and every appeal to compassion as a higher form of law. This is precisely the move Selden mocks. The "compassion" that is exempt from any measure outside itself is the chancellor's foot in a new dress. The compassionate ruling that contradicts the constitution will, over time, hollow out the constitution; the compassionate ruling that contradicts the next compassionate ruling will, over time, hollow out compassion itself. Equity is a friend of law because equity is itself a kind of law: the law of the case, disciplined by the law of the text, both of them disciplined by the prior law of conscience formed in tradition.

Take the warning into the day. When you plead for the bending of a rule, plead it under a rule. When you sit in any seat of judgment — and even the negotiator and the supervising partner sit in such seats — discipline your foot. Walk solicitous with God, as Micheas said yesterday, which means walk in a measure not your own. The mercy and judgment that pleased the Lord are not improvisations. They are the long obedience that learned, over years, how to be merciful without being arbitrary and how to judge without becoming cruel. Selden's jibe should hang above your desk. It is a Protestant joke, but it teaches a Catholic discipline.`,
