    meditation: `At the bottom of every complaint in the old chancery practice was a clause that has survived, in mostly fossilized form, into our modern pleading: the prayer for relief. The word "prayer" is not accidental. The plaintiff is not demanding; he is asking. He is asking a court of conscience to grant him a particular remedy, and the form of the request acknowledges that the granting is not automatic, that the chancellor sits with a discretion the common-law judge did not have, that the answer to the petition will depend on more than the text of the writ. The prayer for relief is the place in the lawsuit where the legal document becomes, however briefly, a liturgical document. It is the place where the lawyer admits he is asking for grace.

The Psalmist gives us the metaphor in one of the loveliest lines in the Psalter. "Mercy and truth have met each other: justice and peace have kissed." The four virtues are not four; they are two pairs, and each pair is the embrace of opposites that the world cannot keep married. Truth, alone, condemns. Mercy, alone, excuses. They meet in the prayer for relief, when the lawyer brings the truth of the case and the mercy of the remedy into the same paragraph, and asks the court to grant what is just in a particular case rather than what is merely owed under a general rule. Justice and peace, similarly, are not the same; the justice that produces no peace is a justice that has failed of its end. The chancery courts existed to perform that kiss. The modern lawyer, in many practices, is the one still tasked with arranging the meeting.

Blackstone, in his great defense of equity, calls it "the soul and spirit of all law." That is a high phrase, and worth pausing over. The body of the law is the text — the statutes, the codes, the precedents, the rules of procedure. The soul of the law is what gives the body its life: the purpose it serves, the conscience it answers to, the human good it was constituted to protect. A body without a soul is a corpse, no matter how well preserved. A statute without equity is enforceable but dead. The chancery, in Blackstone's account, is the institution that keeps the soul present to the body, that breathes purpose back into the rule when the rule has begun to forget.

It matters that the prayer for relief is plural. The old pleading typically ended with a request for specific relief — return of the property, an injunction, an accounting — and then a residual clause: "and for such other and further relief as to the court may seem just and equitable." The residual clause is the open hand. It is the lawyer's acknowledgment that he cannot specify in advance every remedy the case might require, and his trust that the court, sitting in equity, has the imagination and the authority to fashion what the case demands. The clause is small and easy to skim. It is also a confession that the law is not a vending machine: you do not press the button for your remedy and receive it; you ask, and the court answers.

You will draft prayers for relief this week. Many of them will be formulaic, and that is fine; the form has its place. But there are cases in which the prayer is the most important paragraph in the complaint, and you should know how to write one. The first discipline is to ask for what you actually want, not what the form last asked for. The second is to ask for what the court can actually give, in the actual jurisdiction, under the actual cause of action; nothing collapses the prayer faster than the asking for what no court has the power to grant. The third, and the deepest, is to ask in a register that takes seriously the conscience of the court. You are not filing a demand letter; you are entering an institution that, at its origin, was a chapel as much as a court, and that even now retains the form of a petition. The petition has a shape. Honor the shape.

The shape, the Psalmist tells us, is the embrace. Mercy meets truth; justice kisses peace. The prayer for relief is the place in the lawsuit where the lawyer practices that embrace publicly. He asks for the relief that is true to the wrong done — not less, not more — and he asks for it in a form that opens toward peace. He does not ask for the destruction of the defendant; he asks for the restoration of the plaintiff. He does not ask for vengeance; he asks for the remedy that allows the parties, after this is over, to live in the same town. There is a discipline of the imagination required for this. Most lawyers, under client pressure, draft prayers that prosecute. The equitable lawyer drafts prayers that heal.

Consider what this means in a particular case. The contract has been breached; the damages are quantifiable; the client wants every cent. You can plead it that way, and you may have to. But the prayer for relief is also the place where you ask whether specific performance would actually serve your client better than money, whether a constructive trust would protect what damages would only paper over, whether an accounting would establish a baseline that prevents the next dispute. These are equitable remedies. They are remedies of the imagination, fashioned by chancellors over centuries because the common-law remedy of damages, by itself, often fails to do what justice in the case requires. The lawyer who knows them, and who knows when to ask for them, is the lawyer who can pray for the kind of relief the case actually needs.

Pray your prayers for relief carefully this week. The form has a memory you should not lose.`,
