    meditation: `Aquinas gives the famous example. A friend has left his sword with you for safekeeping. He returns one day, raging — perhaps mad, perhaps drunk, perhaps possessed by some grievance that has lost its proportion — and demands the sword back. The law says: restore deposits. The law has no exceptions for moods. And yet to hand him the sword is to make yourself the proximate cause of whatever he does with it. Equity, in this case, is the refusal to hand back the sword. It is not a violation of the law; it is, Aquinas says, the law's own intention rightly perceived. The lawgiver, had he foreseen this case, would have written the exception in. The exception is implicit in the purpose of the rule.

The merciful judge stands daily in this position. He is asked to apply rules that were drafted, sensibly, for the average case. He sees, in front of him, the case the rule did not foresee. He has to decide whether to hand back the sword. The Lord's command in Luke is not, as a lazy reading sometimes takes it, a counsel against judging at all. "Judge not, and you shall not be judged" is spoken to disciples in a moral context, not to officers of a court. The judge of a misdemeanor docket is required by his oath to judge. The question is what kind of judge. Christ's answer is precise: be merciful, as your Father also is merciful. Merciful, not lax. Merciful, not abolitionist. Merciful, in the way that the Father's mercy does not undo His justice but completes it.

Consider what this looks like in a sentencing. The guidelines yield a number. The number is the product of a calculation done by a commission with no knowledge of this defendant, refined by amendments lobbied for by interests that may have nothing to do with him, applied by a probation officer whose report was drafted in an hour. The number is a sword the rule asks the judge to return. Sometimes the number is right; the guidelines exist for a reason; most cases fit reasonably well within the band. Sometimes the number is wrong, and the judge can see, from the bench, that handing it back will be the proximate cause of harm the rule never intended. The merciful judge departs from the guideline. He writes the departure into the record, in language that other judges can read, so that the departure does not become an arbitrariness but a contribution to the slow growth of the law's conscience. He upholds the law by refusing the law's own miscalculation.

You may not be a judge. Most readers will not be. But you stand at sentencing on behalf of someone — defendant, victim, prosecutor's office, parole board, employer considering whether to fire — and the position you take influences what the merciful judge will do. The Christian advocate at sentencing has a particular calling, which is to tell the court the things the guideline cannot know. The defendant's childhood. The mother in the hospital. The job that is waiting if the sentence is short. The pattern of cooperation. The remorse that is real and the remorse that is performed. The cookie-cutter sentencing memorandum is a betrayal here. The guidelines have already supplied the cookie cutter; what the court needs from you is the case, written in enough detail that the judge has something to be merciful with. Mercy without information is the chancellor's foot we feared yesterday. Mercy with information is the discipline of equity at work.

Luke pushes the discipline further: "with the same measure that you shall mete withal, it shall be measured to you again." It is one of the few places where the Gospel makes the legal mind shudder. The measure with which you measure others will be the measure brought to you. The judge who sentences without imagination will be sentenced without imagination. The prosecutor who charges to the maximum without inquiry will be charged to the maximum without inquiry. The advocate who fails to plead the human details of his client will, on a day he hopes will never come, find that no one is pleading the human details of him. The verse is terrifying because it is true. The legal life is not insulated from the moral economy of mercy; it is one of the places that economy is most visibly enacted, and the mercy you spend on your hardest cases is the mercy you are storing up for your own.

Aquinas's sword example contains a discipline against sentimentality, which the merciful judge needs as much as he needs the courage to bend. The discipline is this: mercy is not the same as the wish to be liked, nor the same as the avoidance of difficult outcomes. The mercy of refusing the sword is, for the lender, a hard mercy; he will leave the house angry and may never speak to you again. The mercy of a downward departure may be experienced by the victim as a betrayal, and the merciful judge owes the victim an account of why the law in this case asked something other than what its number said. Mercy that costs nothing is not yet mercy. Mercy that costs the merciful something — the friendship, the easy ruling, the appeal — is mercy that participates, in some small measure, in the costly mercy of the Father, who did not save the world by waiving its laws but by satisfying them in His Son.

Be merciful, as your Father also is merciful. The verb is imperative because the disposition is one we will not arrive at by accident. The pressures of the docket, the pull of the metric, the appetite for the clean case — these will form a different disposition unless we resist. The form of resistance is small: a sentencing memorandum that takes an extra evening, a question to the probation officer that takes an extra phone call, a willingness to depart from the boilerplate when the boilerplate is the sword the case asked you to return. The merciful judge is built in these small acts. So is the merciful advocate.`,
