    meditation: `The negotiation table is the last private court most of your cases will ever see. The litigated case is the exception; the settled case is the rule. Most of the work you do for clients will end not with a verdict but with a signed agreement, drafted late, often in a windowless conference room or on a phone call between two tired lawyers, after the discovery has dragged on too long and the trial date is too close and everyone wants to be done. The agreement will not be reviewed by a judge. It will not be examined by a jury. It will become, between these parties, the law that governs their continued life. The negotiation table is, in this sense, a court of conscience. The chancellor sitting at it is you.

Cicero saw, two thousand years ago, what your week will see in miniature. "Injustices often arise also through chicanery, that is, through an over-subtle and even fraudulent construction of the law." The famous general makes a thirty-day truce and ravages the enemy fields by night, on the technicality that the truce spoke of "days." The text is on his side. The conscience is not. Cicero's verdict is unsparing: such sharp practice, he says, is not to be approved. The proverb he is quoting — summum ius summa iniuria, here in its early form "more law, less justice" — names exactly the temptation the negotiation table puts in front of the careful lawyer.

The temptation is sharper at the table than in the courtroom, because at the table there is no judge to stop you. If you draft an indemnity clause that the other side does not quite understand, the court will not catch it; it will only be discovered, perhaps years later, when the indemnification is triggered and the other party realizes what they signed. If you slip a release of unknown claims into the boilerplate of a settlement, the plaintiff who signed it may not learn what she gave up until she tries, later, to bring the claim she never knew she had. If you negotiate against an unrepresented party — a homeowner, a small contractor, a domestic-violence victim trying to settle a civil matter alongside the criminal case — you have, in effect, become both advocate and chancellor. There is no one else in the room with the legal training to see what you are doing. You are not only the one who can take advantage; you are the only one who can refuse to.

James, with his characteristic bluntness, frames the stakes theologically. "For judgment without mercy to him that hath not done mercy. And mercy exalteth itself above judgment." The verse is sometimes read as a simple promise that the merciful will be treated mercifully and the unmerciful will not. That is part of it. But the second sentence is more striking: mercy exalts itself above judgment. The Greek verb is one of triumph; mercy stands taller than judgment, mercy wins. James is not saying that mercy abolishes judgment. He is saying that, in the final accounting, the merciful action carries a weight the judgmental action does not. The merciful clause in your settlement agreement is louder, before God, than the clever clause. You will not see this in the metrics. You will see it, perhaps, in a stillness that comes when you close the file and know that you did not have to add a sentence to be ashamed of.

Equity at the table looks like several specific habits. It looks like reading the agreement back in the voice of the other party. Does this paragraph mean what you would want it to mean if you were on the other side? It looks like distinguishing the leverage that the case actually gives you from the leverage that the other party's ignorance, exhaustion, or unrepresented status gives you. The first is fair; the second is the chicanery Cicero condemned. It looks like the discipline of explanation: where a clause is unusual, where a waiver is consequential, where a release is broad, you say so plainly, and you give the other side a chance to understand it. You will sometimes lose, by this practice, a few sentences of advantage. You will also sleep.

Equity at the table looks, too, like the willingness to ask the question that lawyers are trained not to ask: what would be just? Lawyers are trained to ask what their client wants and how to maximize the chance of getting it. These are necessary questions; the lawyer who does not ask them is failing his client. But they are not the only questions. There is, somewhere in the night when the deal is almost done, a moment when the careful lawyer asks himself whether the agreement now on the table is something he could defend to a judge if a judge ever saw it, and beyond that, whether it is something he could defend to his confessor or to God. The settlement agreement is, after all, a creature he is bringing into the world. He owes it the same scrutiny he would owe a court order with his name on it.

The hour-billing structure makes this harder. The careful equity-shaped negotiation takes longer than the aggressive one; the client may not want to pay for the extra time, especially when the marginal hour seems to be benefiting the other side. You will have to learn to explain to your clients, when you can, that the settlement that is sharp will, over years, generate the disputes and the reputational damage and the spiritual residue that the merciful settlement will not. You will not always persuade them. But the duty to attempt the persuasion is part of your office. The advocate is not only the one who closes the deal. He is the one who refuses to close the deal that he knows, in the room, was reached by mercy hiding from itself.

Mercy exalteth itself above judgment. Bring it to the table today. The agreement you draft, the clause you decline to insert, the explanation you offer to the unrepresented party, the moment you stop and ask whether what you are about to do is true to the case — these are the chancery work of a profession that does most of its judgment in private. The week began with the rule that fails the case; it ends with the lawyer who, at the small table where most cases are decided, becomes himself the place where the rule remembers its purpose. Equity, in the end, is a name for the kind of person the law needs in the room.`,
